the island of jersey is at the center of  a growing diplomatic row between the uk  and france today  both countries have now dispatched  military vessels there as the dispute  over post-brexit fishing rights  escalates more than 60 fishing boats  have been  surrounding the jersey port of saint  helia although some have been leaving in  the last few minutes  the european commission has called for  calm but says the conditions of the  post-brexit trade agreement  are not being met now french fishermen  are angry because  jersey recently published a list of 41  european boats authorized to fish in  waters around the island but  those boats need to prove that they  fished in jersey waters at least  10 times in the last three years to get  a license  the french say it is a breach of the  agreement with the eu  and now the escalating tension has led  to the deployment  of two royal navy ships and at least one  french patrol vessel  will representatives from the jersey  government and me are meeting some of  the french fishermen our defense and  security correspondent alistair bunkel  has the latest  what comes out of that meeting what has  come out of that meeting alastair well  the meeting was designed to try and have  both sides share their views and to come  to some sort of compromise that would  seem guys that would see this protest  come to an end a protest that has  achieved quite a lot of prominence  around the world i should say the eu as  you saw in my report has been  commenting on it it's also been  discussed at a press conference in  washington at the pentagon  as well but we await to see what  has come out of that meeting and whether  or not the two sides have found  some common ground or not  in the meantime the raw navy warships  remain  on station just off the coast of the  island  of jersey boris johnson who spoke to  senior jersey officials  this morning said that the royal navy  will remain there for as long  as necessary and i suspect  uh there will now be some sort of  examination of the exact wording of  post-brexit agreements  to try and find out who's in the right  and who is in the wrong here but  as i said in my report it's not the  first time that britain and france has  had  disputes over fishing rights and both  sides seem pretty entrenched  in their positions okay alastair thank  you very much
 

